scribing Dunn\'s collaboration on the book "A Natural Legacy: Ecology in Australia," an early textbook on the Australian environment.\n*   It highlights Dunn\'s work as an editor and filmmaker, including the production of documentary films such as "Frame-Up" and "Fighting for Peace."\n*   A notable quote attributed to Dunn is displayed prominently: "A woman needs a man like a fish needs a bicycle," which was later popularized by Gloria Steinem and became a popular slogan among feminists.\n*   The section also mentions Dunn\'s role as the executive director of the New South Wales Writers\' Centre from December 1992 until her resignation in 2008.\n\n**Section 2: Bibliography**\n\n*   This section lists several books authored or edited by Dunn, including:\n    *   "Frame-Up: Who Bombed the Hilton, Who Didn\'t?" (1983)\n    *   "Fighting For Peace: A History of the Australian Women\'s Peace Movement" (Sydney: Film Australia, 1984)\n    *   "A Natural Legacy: Ecology in Australia" (Co-edited) (Sydney: Pergamon, 1979; 1986)\n    *   "Aspirations and Obstacles: Papers from the second Women Into Politics Workshop" (editor) (Sydney: Women Into Politics, 1994)\n    *   "The bioregions of New South Wales: A practical guide to the assessment of their biodiversity" (National Parks and Wildlife Service, 2002)\n\nOverall, the image provides a concise overview of Dunn\'s career and contributions to literature and filmmaking in Australia.', 'similarity score': 0.45090964436531067}, {'frames/frame_1902': 'frames/frame_1902', 'text': 'The image shows a news article featuring two photographs, with the left photo depicting a woman in a black dress standing in front of a blue door and the right photo showing a woman in a black jacket and hat holding a microphone, accompanied by a caption that reads "Ruth England (left), and Sara Outen - who became the first woman to row solo across the Indian Ocean in 2009".', 'similarity score': 0.45050472021102905}, {'frames/frame_1321': 'frames/frame_